    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The forelimb of the Maiella antiform and the leading edge of the thrust sheet display a complex system of faults with a wide variety of orientation, kinematics and distribution in platform carbonates. This contribution summarizes this rich structural assemblage with an emphasis on their formation mechanisms and significance.\r\n\r\nWe identified a system of normal faults trending sub parallel to the strike of the mechanical beds. These faults formed by a combination of slip across three major sets of pressure solution structures and splay pressure solutions. We also identified two major sets of strike-slip faults; one left lateral nearly perpendicular to the frontal thrust, and the other which is right lateral diverging from the first one by angle comparable to a typical splay angle. These two sets of strike-slip faults in an apparent conjugate pattern can form by sequential shearing of cross pressure solutions and the related splay pressure solutions Finally, we mapped four zones, sub parallel to the frontal thrust, which can be characterized as kink folds with intensely deformed steep forelimbs facing to the east. Major faults within these zones have a reverse/thrust kinematics with vergence to the east, and take advantage of all the existing weaknesses to brecciate the rock in elongated pockets.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
